## Subcommands
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

`compose.mk` can turn any target into a **subcommand-style CLI** -- the
`<prog> <subcommand> <args..>` shape you know from tools like `git` or `docker` --
in a single line, with usage/help generated for free.

The result is a normal entrypoint you invoke as `./yourtool.mk <subcommand> [args..]`
(or, for a project Makefile, run under the interpreter).  The built-in
[`cmk` CLI]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/compiler) is itself built this way.

### The Interface
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

Two pieces: an **entrypoint** that calls `mk.subcommands.enter`, and one **handler
target per subcommand**.

```Makefile
# the entrypoint -- one line makes `greet` a subcommand CLI
greet:; $(call mk.subcommands.enter)

# one handler per subcommand
.greet.world:;     printf 'hello world\n'      # non-parametric: takes no argument
.greet.hello/%:;   printf 'hello, %s!\n' "${*}" # parametric: `%` is the first argument
```

* A **handler** is named `.<namespace>.<subcommand>`.  The namespace defaults to the
  entrypoint's name (`greet` above), so handlers live under `.greet.*`.
* `.<ns>.<sub>` is **non-parametric** -- it takes no argument.
* `.<ns>.<sub>/%` is **parametric** -- the next CLI word becomes its `%` stem, and any
  further words arrive in the `argv` environment variable.
* The subcommand list and the **bare-form default** (what `greet` with no subcommand,
  or an unrecognized first word, falls back to) are detected automatically from your
  handlers; usage/help is generated from them.

Everything is optional and overridable via keyword arguments:

| kwarg | meaning | default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `namespace` | one or more (space-separated) namespaces to search | the target name |
| `subs` | the subcommand list | reflected from the handlers |
| `default` | the bare-form fallback subcommand | the first subcommand |
| `sep` | separator between namespace and subcommand in a handler name | `.` |

```Makefile
# e.g. force a specific default
greet:; $(call mk.subcommands.enter, default=hello)
```

### Example
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

A complete `greet` CLI -- two non-parametric subcommands (`world`, `me`) and one
parametric (`hello`):

{{site.embed_demo('demos/subcommands.mk', github=github, bottom_right=site.link_mirror('demos/cmk/subcommands.cmk'))}}

Using it:

```bash {.cli_example}
# non-parametric subcommands
$ ./demos/subcommands.mk greet world
$ ./demos/subcommands.mk greet me

# parametric subcommand (the word after `hello` is the argument)
$ ./demos/subcommands.mk greet hello bob

# no subcommand (or `help`) prints generated usage
$ ./demos/subcommands.mk greet
$ ./demos/subcommands.mk greet help
```

Generated usage lists each subcommand, marking the ones that accept an argument:

```bash {.cli_output}
greet // USAGE: greet <subcommand> [args..]
  ├┈world
  ├┈me
  ╰┈hello <arg> [args..]
```

### CMK-lang Equivalent
<hr style="width:95%;border-bottom:1px dashed black;">

In [CMK-lang]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/compiler), the entrypoint is written as a
**decorator** -- `ᝏsubcommands` on the line above the target -- instead of a `$(call ..)`
recipe.  Keyword arguments are the same (e.g. `ᝏsubcommands(default=hello)`).

{{site.embed_demo('demos/cmk/subcommands.cmk', type='cmk', github=github, bottom_right=site.link_mirror('demos/subcommands.mk'))}}

!!! tip "See also"
    The `namespace` keyword accepts *several* namespaces, searched in order -- which lets
    you layer subcommands the way a class hierarchy layers methods.  See the
    [Subcommands as OOP tutorial]({{mkdocs.site_relative_url}}/demos/oop) for that.
